# `not`, on a scan assertion — the shape the dogfood suite uses to prove the scanner FINDS things.
#
# 21 of the corpus's 96 scan assertions are negated, every one of them in `tflw-acceptance/
# security/`, where the deliberately-vulnerable targets live. The old SCANS form could not write
# this at all: its `expectSpec()` had no `negated` field.
#
# In the pane, `not` lives behind the row's `⋯` — and a row that already uses it draws it **open**,
# with no gesture required. Compare the two tests below: one is open on arrival, one is not.

@security
test "a page that should be clean, asserted the ordinary way"
  api GET /
  expect status equals 200
  expect response has no critical security violations

@security @inverted
test "…and the same page asserted the other way, which is how you test the scanner"
  api GET /
  expect status equals 200
  # `not has no …` reads badly out loud and is exactly right: *it is not the case that this has no
  # critical violations*, i.e. **find me one**. Against a healthy storefront this FAILS, which is
  # the point — press ▶ and read the verdict.
  expect response not has no critical security violations
